 "source": "Numerics/Data/Statistics/Autocorrelation.cs; the 200-point series and the lag-0..23 covariance/lag-1..23 partial-autocorrelation literals are scraped verbatim from Test_Autocorrelation.cs's Test_Covariance and Test_Partial (both asserted there at 1e-4 tolerance against R's acf()).",
 "reference": "Numerics @ 2a0357a. The correlation type is already cross-checked (against Fourier.Autocorrelation, not Autocorrelation.Function directly) by fixtures/special_functions/fourier.json's Fourier.autocorrelation_at, so this file pins only what that one does not: the covariance and partial types, and correlation_confidence_interval. Args convention: method 'autocorrelation' flattens the {lag, value} pairs row-major (dims = {n, 2}, matching numerics/support/toolbox_runner.hpp's run_spectra arm), so an assertion's index selects a FLAT position -- index = 2*lag + 1 reads the value column at that lag. Test_CorrelationConfidenceInterval (sizes {20,30,40,50,50,60} x intervals {0.90,0.95,0.99}) is a computed-comparison test with no fixed literals to transcribe (it asserts CorrelationConfidenceInterval against a freshly-computed Normal.StandardZ call, not a hardcoded number), and Numerics.Distributions.Normal.StandardZ is itself already oracle-pinned elsewhere in this repo's fixtures, so the confidence-interval expected values here were independently recomputed in Python (Python's stdlib statistics.NormalDist().inv_cdf) and confirmed reproducible against the real C# Autocorrelation.CorrelationConfidenceInterval by tools/verify_oracles.py, per corehydro CLAUDE.md rule 3. The duplicate size=50 case in the C# test is de-duplicated here (it is the same input run twice).",
